**Theorem 9.3 (sharp endpoint minimum for actual low live labels).**
For every parameter in Theorem 1.4, at least four actual low live-bearing
labels are necessary for height optimality, and four suffice. Equivalently
the minimum of this count among original height-optimal sharp-cost
instruments is exactly four. This is not a claim that every four-label
instrument is optimal, or that all optimizers have only four labels.

Proof. If an original actual record attains the global value, its
analytically refined product/failure upper bound from Proposition 2.2
must attain that value too. Otherwise the actual record's TV would be
strictly smaller. The unique grouped optimum and the complete contact
zero sets in Theorems 4.3 and 5.1 force all four distinct contact rays,
with the four strictly positive masses of Lemma 3.2. A single actual
low live-bearing label supplies at most one such ray by Lemma 9.2.
High labels supply only $D$ live products; supported failure-only
labels supply neither these low live rays nor winding coordinates.
Thus three or fewer actual low live-bearing labels cannot attain the
optimum, even with arbitrary complex multiplicity and stochastic report
processing. Analytic report or height-sign refinements do not create a
second coefficient ray within a coarse actual label.

Conversely Definitions 8.1 and EP.30 use exactly four distinct actual
low labels, with full two-dimensional fibres and their respective fixed
whole-fibre decoders. Theorems 8.2 and Proposition 8.3 prove ALL
complex/reference/Borel, twelve-history, TP, $I_5$, contrast and acquired
worst-history identities for this ONE construction. It attains the
matching global value, so four suffice. Countably many positive label
